✅ Java switch Statement: Old vs New (Java 8 vs Java 14+)
Java has improved the switch statement significantly in newer versions (Java 14+), making it more concise, flexible, and expression-oriented.

🔶 1. Old switch Syntax (Java 7 and earlier)

String day = "MONDAY";
String type;

switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY":
    case "TUESDAY":
    case "WEDNESDAY":
    case "THURSDAY":
    case "FRIDAY":
        type = "Weekday";
        break;
    case "SATURDAY":
    case "SUNDAY":
        type = "Weekend";
        break;
    default:
        type = "Invalid";
}
System.out.println(type);
🔸 Drawbacks:

Requires break to prevent fall-through

Verbose

Cannot return a value directly (used for side effects)

✅ 2. Enhanced switch Expression (Java 14+)

String day = "MONDAY";

String type = switch (day) {
    case "MONDAY", "TUESDAY", "WEDNESDAY", "THURSDAY", "FRIDAY" -> "Weekday";
    case "SATURDAY", "SUNDAY" -> "Weekend";
    default -> "Invalid";
};

System.out.println(type);
🔸 Benefits:

switch becomes an expression (can return a value directly)

No break needed

Use of -> arrow syntax

Safer and cleaner

Can use yield for multiline blocks

🧪 3. Multiline Case Blocks with yield

String result = switch (score) {
    case 10, 9 -> "Excellent";
    case 8, 7 -> {
        System.out.println("Good but can improve");
        yield "Good";
    }
    default -> "Average";
};
🔄 Summary Comparison
| Feature                   | Old Switch                      | New Switch (Java 14+) |
| ------------------------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------- |
| Syntax                    | `case ...: break;`              | `case ... ->`         |
| Multiple labels per case  | ✅ (repeated cases)              | ✅ (comma-separated)   |
| Need `break`              | ✅ Yes                           | ❌ No                  |
| Used as expression        | ❌ No                            | ✅ Yes                 |
| `yield` for block returns | ❌ No                            | ✅ Yes                 |
| Null-safe                 | ❌ Throws `NullPointerException` | ❌ Still throws NPE    |


✅ When to Use New Switch
Use the enhanced switch:

When assigning values based on cases

To improve readability

When you want to avoid accidental fall-through bugs

